WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:05.300
Deadpool 2 was filled with twists and surprises,
like the unexpected demise of the entire X-Force

00:00:05.300 --> 00:00:05.820
team.

00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:09.440
But maybe the biggest shock of all was the
reveal of the biggest bad guy of them all

00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:10.320
- the Juggernaut.

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:14.960
So how did Ryan Reynolds and company manage
to keep Juggernaut a surprise for so long?

00:00:15.060 --> 00:00:17.120
And how exactly did they make Juggernaut at
all?

00:00:17.380 --> 00:00:21.280
Here's a look at some surprising secrets the
biggest fans may not know about Juggernaut

00:00:21.280 --> 00:00:22.160
in Deadpool 2.

00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:24.040
Top secret project

00:00:25.040 --> 00:00:29.890
Deadpool 2 kept a lot of its biggest jokes
a secret, like the unexpected cameos of superstars

00:00:29.890 --> 00:00:31.390
Brad Pitt and Matt Damon.

00:00:31.390 --> 00:00:36.180
And keeping spoilers off the internet was
no easy task, as writer and executive producer

00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:39.860
Paul Wernick revealed in the special features
on Deadpool 2's home release.

00:00:40.380 --> 00:00:42.760
"Sometimes the secrecy thing gets ridiculous.

00:00:42.760 --> 00:00:48.040
You know, our sides came in red because apparently you can't photocopy red."

00:00:48.040 --> 00:00:51.400
And then she shreds them. It's like working in the CIA sometimes."

00:00:51.820 --> 00:00:56.260
But the biggest source of leaks turned out
incredibly random, as Juggernaut's surprise

00:00:56.260 --> 00:01:01.470
appearance was almost spoiled by another surprise
appearance, when legendary Guns n' Roses guitarist

00:01:01.470 --> 00:01:03.170
Slash turned up on set.

00:01:03.170 --> 00:01:05.020
Reynolds said it was a security nightmare.

00:01:05.020 --> 00:01:10.980
"Suddenly all of these pictures of Slash were on people's phone, with like, Juggernaut in the background."

00:01:10.980 --> 00:01:15.000
"I'm like, 'Guys, you can't post any of those! At least Photoshop the crap out of them!'"

00:01:15.860 --> 00:01:16.380
The blob

00:01:17.240 --> 00:01:22.010
Even though Juggernaut was created using CGI,
the actors on the set still needed a reference

00:01:22.010 --> 00:01:23.480
to play off of during filming.

00:01:23.480 --> 00:01:24.480
The solution?

00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:25.880
A giant padded dummy.

00:01:25.880 --> 00:01:29.920
It helped the actors nail their performances,
but it also led to even more problems keeping

00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:33.110
the Juggernaut's appearance secret, as writer
Rhett Reese explained.

00:01:33.320 --> 00:01:37.520
"If you've seen the physical reference, it is DEFINITELY Juggernaut.

00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:42.000
It's like his outline, you can see his helmet and then below him is this amorphous blob."

00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:43.360
The big problem?

00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:45.920
That signature helmet, which couldn't belong
to anyone else.

00:01:46.340 --> 00:01:52.440
"Anyone who has any background in comics, if they were on set that day, would spy that particular getup and go

00:01:52.440 --> 00:01:53.980
"Hah! Juggernaut's going to be in the movie."

00:01:54.460 --> 00:01:55.580
The man in the iron mask

00:01:56.340 --> 00:02:00.520
Since an amorphous blob wouldn't have been
particularly scary for audiences - no offense

00:02:00.520 --> 00:02:05.290
to fans of The Blob - the folks behind Deadpool
decided to use CGI to animate Juggernaut.

00:02:05.290 --> 00:02:08.940
And when we say it was the folks behind Deadpool,
we mean that the role was actually played

00:02:08.940 --> 00:02:12.500
by the team of director David Leitch 
and Ryan Reynolds himself.

00:02:12.920 --> 00:02:16.980
Yep, it's true: Reynolds provided the voice
and did the motion capture for the body, while

00:02:16.989 --> 00:02:19.840
Leitch did the motion capture for the Juggernaut's
facial expressions.

00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:23.770
And the reason was simple: it was just more
efficient to do it themselves rather than

00:02:23.770 --> 00:02:26.859
waste time bringing another actor up to speed
on the role.

00:02:26.860 --> 00:02:32.380
"He's definitely a mashup of us and that's really born out of the need to work efficiently."

00:02:32.900 --> 00:02:34.720
"It's sort of a weird amalgamation."

00:02:34.720 --> 00:02:42.200
"This incredible team of renderers are the guys that bring him to life. That one took a village,  that was all of us jumping in and making the Juggernaut."

00:02:43.180 --> 00:02:43.780
Black Bob

00:02:44.420 --> 00:02:48.200
Juggernaut's appearance in the movie was also
nearly revealed thanks to the casting of a

00:02:48.209 --> 00:02:52.030
completely different character — Jack Kesy as Black Tom Cassidy.

00:02:52.030 --> 00:02:55.319
In the comics, of course, Black Tom and Juggernaut
are partners.

00:02:55.319 --> 00:02:59.329
So in order to keep the secret, Fox decided
to use a different code name when making the

00:02:59.329 --> 00:03:00.579
casting announcement.

00:03:00.580 --> 00:03:03.380
Unfortunately, according to Wernick, that
didn't quite work.

00:03:03.700 --> 00:03:06.700
"It was released that Jack was playing, I think it was Black Bob."

00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:14.860
"Which was a terrible idea. Ya know, they couldn't have come up with anything other than Black Blank? Couldn't it have been like, 'Larry'"?

00:03:15.640 --> 00:03:19.840
As it turned out, Black Tom barely even appeared
in Deadpool 2, meaning this secret ultimately

00:03:19.860 --> 00:03:22.860
almost leaked on his behalf for no real reason.

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:23.500
Whoops!

00:03:24.140 --> 00:03:25.580
The irresistible force

00:03:26.380 --> 00:03:30.439
During the film's climax, Juggernaut was seemingly
stopped for good when the good guys stuck

00:03:30.439 --> 00:03:32.950
an electrical cord into… the wrong socket.

00:03:35.180 --> 00:03:39.620
Well, before you get too weepy for poor ol
Jugs, though, take heart, as eagle-eyed fans

00:03:39.620 --> 00:03:43.819
noticed that in the film's final scene, Juggernaut
appears to be alive after all.

00:03:43.819 --> 00:03:45.919
So what does this mean for Juggernaut's future?

00:03:45.919 --> 00:03:49.999
Nobody knows for sure, but chances are, we
haven't seen the last of him.

00:03:49.999 --> 00:03:51.909
After all, it's pretty hard to stop a Juggernaut.

